<LessonLayout title="Anointing of the Sick" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">Where is God when I suffer? Has He abandoned me?</p>


<p>
In the fourth century, a young Roman woman named Fabiola lost her husband. Then she lost her faith. She wandered through the streets of Rome, restless and empty. Then she met a priest who did not give her arguments but mercy. She converted, sold all her possessions, and built the first public hospital in the West. She personally cared for the sick, the dying, the abandoned. When asked why she spent her days with the suffering, she replied: "I look at Christ in them, and He looks at me through them." The Church has always known that sickness is not a punishment but a mystery. The Anointing of the Sick is not a last rite for the dying. It is a sacrament of healing for the living, a touch of Christ for those who suffer.
</p>


</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Understanding the Mystery</h2>

